{
  "case_id": "2019-19-631",
  "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:42 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (William P. Barr, Attorney General)",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STEWART: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: In 1991, Congress enacted the TCPA's basic restriction on the placement of automated calls to cell phones."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Thank you, Chief Mr. Stewart, it would seem a bit odd, as you suggest, that we sever the exception, but, here, it doesn't seem -- this remedy doesn't seem to give anything to Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 13,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Counsel, I don't see how you can escape a content-based distinction. If the content is a debt owed to the government, that's the content of the message, you owe the government for a student loan or whatever, then the call is okay. But, if the message is, please contribute to our political organization, it's banned. So it's based on what the message is."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice KAGAN",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Could we go back to the -- what you started with? You said that there was no right way to think about how to analyze this question, that we could either apply constitutional analysis to the automated-call restriction or we could apply it to the exemption for government debt."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Good morning, counsel. Some of my colleagues have already noted the irony of a First Amendment challenge leading to the suppression of more speech as a remedy."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Let me come at it from yet another angle, and that's the separation of powers. The government's remedy proposed here is essentially that we should suppose or -- or reimagine that Congress would have preferred a regime in which more speech is suppressed than one in which less is suppressed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice KAVANAUGH",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the government-debt exception is almost certainly content-based, at least for me. And I just wanted as a matter of housekeeping, you don't argue that it could satisfy strict scrutiny, correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Roman MARTINEZ",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0001-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Federal courts cannot fix First Amendment violations by making more speech illegal."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Roman MARTINEZ",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0001-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the 2015 exception really does undermine that because it's not getting at like -- it -- it's not trying to exempt the least intrusive of privacy speech available."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Roman MARTINEZ",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0001-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the fundamental problem here is the invalidity of the restriction. And I think that the -- even before you get to any severability inquiry about intent, we have to be very careful and specific about what is unconstitutional about the statute."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: I'll give you a hypothetical. Suppose this statute was written in a slightly different way and it exempted any calls between the holder of a government debt and the debtor. Would strict scrutiny apply?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Let me see if I -- if I've got at least that second point, my hands around it. The argument is that maybe the first Congress that enacted the original statute"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Let me just -- let me make sure I have you exactly right. The underlying restriction, if there had never been a government-debt exception -- let me phrase it that way. If there had never been a government-debt exception, is the underlying restriction unconstitutional?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-19-631",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "976ed1a4de13c226b15de2ce8e6f99d5758b697fda7f4ce6fc8fd2dae3834195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-976ed1a4de13-0003-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 76,
        "start": 76
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "(Whereupon, at 12:55 p.m., the case was submitted.)"
    },
